maniT Capability-Based Security Demo
=====================================
Authored by: Manish Jagdish Thatte

================================================
  1. Process Ring Levels — The Ternary Privilege Model
================================================
  thatteOS three-ring model:
    Ring + (Kernel):  full hardware access, memory management
    Ring 0 (Service): device drivers, system daemons
    Ring - (User):    application code, sandboxed

  Running processes:
    PID 0 [kernel] ring=+  =>  Kernel  (+)
    PID 1 [disk_driver] ring=0  =>  Service (0)
    PID 2 [netstack] ring=0  =>  Service (0)
    PID 3 [editor] ring=-  =>  User    (-)
    PID 4 [shell] ring=-  =>  User    (-)

================================================
  2. Capability Tokens — Trit-Encoded Permissions
================================================
  Permission encoding:
    + => full access   (read/write/execute)
    0 => partial       (read-only / check)
    - => no access     (denied / revoked)

  Allocated capabilities:
    Cap#1  resource="/dev/mem"  R=+ W=+ X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 0
    Cap#2  resource="/dev/disk"  R=+ W=+ X=-  min_ring=0  owner=PID 1
    Cap#3  resource="/home/data"  R=+ W=+ X=-  min_ring=-  owner=PID 3
    Cap#4  resource="/dev/net"  R=+ W=0 X=-  min_ring=0  owner=PID 2

================================================
  3. TMIN2 Gate Permission Checking
================================================
  TMIN2 gate: access = process_ring tand required_ring
  If result == required_ring: GRANT.  Otherwise: DENY.
  One gate. One cycle. Complete security check.

  Access matrix (process ring vs required ring):

                     Required Ring
                     Kernel(+)  Service(0)  User(-)
    Kernel (+):    GRANT    GRANT    GRANT
    Service(0):    DENY     GRANT    GRANT
    User   (-):    DENY     DENY     GRANT

  Key insight: kernel (+) can access everything (TMIN2 always passes).
  User (-) can only access user-level resources.
  Service (0) can access service and user, but not kernel.
  This is exactly the principle of least privilege, in one trit.

================================================
  4. Capability Attenuation — Privileges Can Only Decrease
================================================
  Attenuation uses tand (min) — the privilege can only go down.
  A kernel process can give a service process a read-only view.
  The service process CANNOT escalate it back to read-write.

  Original capability (kernel, full access):
    Cap#10  resource="/dev/mem"  R=+ W=+ X=+  min_ring=+  owner=PID 0
  After attenuation (read-only, given to service):
    Cap#10  resource="/dev/mem"  R=+ W=- X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 1
  After further attenuation (partial read, given to user):
    Cap#10  resource="/dev/mem"  R=0 W=- X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 3

  Attempting to ESCALATE (user tries to grant write):
  Result (tand prevents escalation):
    Cap#10  resource="/dev/mem"  R=0 W=- X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 3
  Write permission is still -, because tand(-, +) = -.
  Escalation is MATHEMATICALLY IMPOSSIBLE with tand.

================================================
  5. Capability Delegation — Transferring Access
================================================
  A process can delegate a capability to another process.
  The delegated capability can only have EQUAL or FEWER permissions.

  Step 1: Kernel (PID 0) creates network capability:
    Cap#20  resource="/dev/net"  R=+ W=+ X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 0
  Step 2: Kernel delegates to netstack (PID 2), read-full, write-partial:
    Cap#20  resource="/dev/net"  R=+ W=0 X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 2
  Step 3: Netstack delegates to browser (PID 4), read-only:
    Cap#20  resource="/dev/net"  R=+ W=- X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 4

  Delegation chain:
    Kernel (+, +, -)  --attenuate-->  Service (+, 0, -)  --attenuate-->  User (+, -, -)
    Permissions can only decrease at each step.
    The tand gate enforces this at the hardware level.

  System capability table:
    Cap#20  resource="/dev/net"  R=+ W=+ X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 0
    Cap#20  resource="/dev/net"  R=+ W=0 X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 2
    Cap#20  resource="/dev/net"  R=+ W=- X=-  min_ring=+  owner=PID 4

================================================
  6. Capability Revocation — Setting All Trits to -
================================================
  To revoke a capability, set all permission trits to -.
  This is a single trit-fill operation (tnot of all-+ is all-).

  Before revocation:
    Cap#30  resource="/home/secret"  R=+ W=+ X=-  min_ring=-  owner=PID 3
  After revocation:
    Cap#30  resource="/home/secret"  R=- W=- X=-  min_ring=-  owner=PID 3

  Verification: all access checks now fail.
    Kernel  tries read: perm=-, effective=-  => DENIED
    Service tries read: perm=-, effective=-  => DENIED
    User    tries read: perm=-, effective=-  => DENIED

================================================
  7. Why Three Rings are Sufficient
================================================
  x86 has four rings (0, 1, 2, 3).
  In practice, only rings 0 and 3 are used.
  Rings 1 and 2 are wasted — complexity for nothing.

  thatteOS has three rings (+, 0, -).
  ALL THREE are actively used:
    + (Kernel):  memory management, interrupt handling
    0 (Service): device drivers, file system, network stack
    - (User):    applications, shells, user code

  Three levels map directly to three operational concerns:
    + = hardware control  (must not be accessible from outside)
    0 = system services   (mediate between hardware and apps)
    - = user computation  (sandboxed, least privilege)

  In hardware:
    Binary ring check:   subtract + compare (multi-cycle)
    Ternary ring check:  one trit comparison (one TMIN2 gate)
    The security boundary is enforced in a single clock cycle.

  Capability attenuation: tand(current, desired) = min
  Privilege can only flow downward: + -> 0 -> -
  Escalation is impossible without a new grant from a higher ring.

Demo complete.
